03 — Portainer Setup

What is this for?
Portainer is a web-based Docker management UI. It runs as a container itself and lets you manage all other containers, images, volumes, and networks from a browser instead of the command line.

Prerequisites
Ubuntu Server VM is running and you are SSH'd in
Docker is installed (see below if not)

Installing Docker
If Docker is not already installed on the VM, run:

   sudo apt update
   sudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg
   sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
   curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
   sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg

   echo \
     "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] \
     https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \
     $(. /etc/os-release && echo "$VERSION_CODENAME") stable" | \
     sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null

   sudo apt update
   sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin

Verify Docker is running:

   sudo systemctl status docker

Add your user to the Docker group so you don't need sudo every time:

   sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
   newgrp docker

Creating the Portainer Volume
Portainer needs a persistent volume to store its data:

   docker volume create portainer_data

Running Portainer
Deploy Portainer as a container:

   docker run -d \
     -p 8000:8000 \
     -p 9443:9443 \
     --name portainer \
     --restart=always \
     -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
     -v portainer_data:/data \
     portainer/portainer-ce:latest

Flags explained:
-p 9443:9443    HTTPS web UI port
-p 8000:8000    Agent tunnel port
--restart=always    Portainer starts automatically on reboot
-v /var/run/docker.sock    Gives Portainer access to manage Docker

Accessing Portainer
Open a browser and navigate to:

   https://10.0.0.x:9443

Replace 10.0.0.x with your Ubuntu Server VM's IP address.
You will get a browser warning about the certificate — this is expected, click Advanced and proceed.

First Time Setup
Create an admin username and password when prompted
Select Get Started to manage the local Docker environment
You should see the local environment appear on the home screen

Verify It is Working
You should see the Portainer dashboard with your local Docker environment
The container list should show the Portainer container itself running
From this point on, you can deploy and manage all other containers from the Portainer UI

